[{:db/id 17592186379305 :question/problem "Given that $L{{e}^{at}}=\\frac{1}{s-a}$ for $s>a$, find the Laplace transform of\n$g(t)={\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{-3τ}\\,\\text{d}τ$\nfor $s>0.$" :question/worked-solution "Evaluate the integral first:\n\n$$g(t)=\\int_0^t e^{-3\\tau}\\,d\\tau=\\frac{1-e^{-3t}}3.$$\n\nFor $s>0$, both exponential integrals converge, and\n\n$$\\mathcal L\\{g\\}(s)=\\frac13\\left(\\frac1s-\\frac1{s+3}\\right)=\\frac1{s(s+3)}.$$\n\nThis is option a. The integrand $g(t)$ tends to $1/3$. In fact, for $t\\ge1$, $g(t)\\ge(1-e^{-3})/3>0$, so the defining Laplace integral diverges for every real $s\\le0$. Thus the maximal real convergence domain is $s>0$. The original source's $s>-3$ is the convergence condition for $e^{-3t}$, not for its integral; extending the rational expression to negative $s$ does not extend the transform." :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
